; ============================================================================
; GROUP APPLY FAILURE -- STAGE (AIF-160)
;
; Opens both members, begins the transaction, and STAGES both UPDATEs. It
; stops there, deliberately, leaving the SQL transaction OPEN across the script
; boundary -- DOTSCRIPT runs in this process, so the areas, the mode and the
; transaction all survive into the commit script.
;
; WHY THE SPLIT EXISTS, and it was measured rather than assumed. The first cut
; of this arm planted the foreign record lock BEFORE the work script and got:
;
;   SQLSEL: UPDATE refused -- record locked.
;
; SQLSEL takes the record lock when it STAGES a change, not when the commit
; applies it. A lock present before the statement runs is therefore caught by
; fail-fast, the transaction never enlists that table, and no group is ever
; decided -- which is the owner's refuse-on-contention contract working exactly
; as ruled, and the wrong moment for this measurement.
;
; The window this arm needs is AFTER staging and BEFORE apply, so the fence is
; planted between these two scripts.
; ============================================================================

SET ECHO OFF
STOP_ON_ERROR OFF
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX
SET PATH INDEXES INDEXES/SANDBOX
SET PATH LMDB LMDB/SANDBOX

FORMULA "GRPFAIL-STAGE-BEGIN"

SELECT 1
USE GRPFAIL1
SELECT 2
USE GRPFAIL2
SELECT 1

SET MODE SQL
BEGIN
UPDATE GRPFAIL1 SET MARK = 'AFTER' WHERE ID = 1
UPDATE GRPFAIL2 SET MARK = 'AFTER' WHERE ID = 1

FORMULA "GRPFAIL-STAGE-END"
